A||N||11||MikeSchaefer||How to make my 84 Steer like an 85 GT or better?||||5||
Z||000000||MikeSchaefer||09-18-2000||02:45 PM||||Hi,<BR>I posted this to the general Fiero chat, with few responses, so I'll try it here:<P>  I used to drive an 85 GT, now I have an 84. I was wandering what modifications I could make to my 84 to make it steer more like my 85 GT did. I know there are several <BR>differences in parts under there. Is there a way to swap parts from other fiero's steering rack (or whatever is needed) to improve my 84? It just doesn't seem to turn as tightly and accurately. I have complete access to 87 coupe parts if they are needed. I hope someone can understand what I'm asking <BR>Thanks,<BR>Mike Schaefer  <A HREF="http://surf.to/fiero" TARGET=_blank>http://surf.to/fiero</A>  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||ThatRickGuy||09-18-2000||06:25 PM||||I didn't think there were any major changes until '88. I'd say start investing in a suspention/steering job. New shocks, struts, check the tie rods, throw a sway bar on the back end, get one of those heavy ones in the front. she'll handle better again.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-18-2000||08:35 PM||||What I said before,,,, <P>Just replacing shocks and struts will do allot for it.<P>check the RPO codes for the 87. unless it has codes indicating one of the special handling packages you're wasting your time.<P>You'd also want to find the 3 turn rack. You need the whole rack.<P><BR>To add,,,,,,,<P>You need the front and rear springs, the steering rack, and maybe the sway bar from a 85-87 car with one of the handling packages. I.E. A SC SE or GT. There may be a few base coupes that where optioned with the upgraded suspension but the only way you will know for sure is run thru the RPO codes.<P>even after that you'll likely need to replace parts that are worn. bushings ball joints and the like.<P>One "cheap" thing you can do is put pollyurethane or even solid bushings into the cradle mounts. That will stabilize the car considerably.||||reg||1
Z||000003||MikeSchaefer||09-18-2000||08:42 PM||||Thanks again for the response Ogre,  Do you know what RPO the 3-turn rack is?  <BR>Mike||||reg||6
Z||000004||Phil||09-18-2000||10:12 PM||||Did 85 GTs have rear sway bars?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||bHooper||09-18-2000||10:28 PM||||i thought only the 88 Gt's and Formula's had the rear swaybar.  i had to add one to my '86.5.<P>it makes a big difference... i haven't had mine on, since i dropped the engine last spring, and it handle's like a bowl of jello!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||bHooper||09-18-2000||10:32 PM||||i thought only the 88 Gt's and Formula's had the rear swaybar.  i had to add one to my '86.5.<P>it makes a big difference... i haven't had mine on, since i dropped the engine last spring, and it handle's like a bowl of jello!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||Xtreme Fiero's||09-19-2000||06:36 PM||||Upgrade the shocks, Lower the car if you like, and get front & rear sway bars. Our 84's don't have a rear sway bar, that causes our cars to push alot more than GT's and 85-87 car's with the WS6 package. both of my 84's have the WS6 package and still have a tiny front sway bar and no rear bar. The fiero store sell's front & rear sway bars for about $300 for the set. just be sure not to make the rear larger than the front. also if you raise the tire pressur in the rear about 5-10 psi. the car will get alot tighter & sway less.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||09-19-2000||06:48 PM||||The only Fiero's that came stock with a rear sway bar were the '88 GT and Formula.  SFAIK, the front sway bar's were the same size on all Fiero's.  hmm.. I'll have to take and compare my 84 sc to my 86 GT...<P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 150,000 miles and counting!       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">    <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-19-2000||11:30 PM||||I forgot tire pressure...... I run my current tires all at 32PSI instead of the 30 marked on the door. The last set was best at 33. (A 1 PSI change is several pounds of spring rate.) It varries some from one tire style to another what's best for what kind of driving. <B>NEVER EVER put more air in the tire than it's marked maximum inflation!</B>||||reg||1
Z||000010||MikeSchaefer||09-20-2000||04:23 PM||||Hi,<BR>Thanks for all of the responses guys!  Here is some more information on my "new" 84 Fiero that I should have probably stated before.  It has been lowered 1.5" (I believe that was the size) using Fiero Store Sport springs, and has 15" rims.  Is this combination something that I should keep (in other words, will the mods you guys mentioned still make the differences or would I have to loose the springs or rims?)<BR>I'm suspension stupid I guess, but I do understand the oversteer/understeer issues related to different sized and placed sway bars.<BR>Thanks again,<BR>Michael Schaefer||||reg||1
Z||000011||Xtreme Fiero's||09-20-2000||10:45 PM||||Just an idea but it may be in your head, no joke. my friends 88GT seems like it can out corner my 84 SE when we have raced and I can stick to his bumper no problem, we have even drive each others cars, we think since his car can out rum me on a straightaway he is hiting the brakes  more than me upseting the balance of his car causing us to feel higher G forces. while I can stay on it all the way through but we sill turn in at the same speed. my 84 has stock shocks & springs, and stock 14" wheels W/ 215/60/14's his was stock at the time to He had no cat.<P>i hope this made some sense.||||reg||1
